<%
    // WHAT A BLOCKED ACTION LOOKS LIKE, AND WHY IT IS SHOWN RATHER THAN HIDDEN.
    //
    // A DEMO SESSION CANNOT WRITE. The server refuses every mutating request for one
    // (see `refuseWritesForDemoSessions` in lib/auth/routes.js), which is the control
    // that matters: a POST can be sent by hand, and this link leaves the owner's hands.
    //
    // This partial is the VISIBLE half, and it shows the actions disabled rather than
    // removing them. A console with the buttons missing reads as broken software to
    // somebody seeing it for the first time, and the reader's next thought is that the
    // tool is unfinished rather than that they are looking at a demo. Saying so plainly
    // costs one line and removes the ambiguity.
    //
    // The sentence describing what forgeright does is WORDED THE SAME HERE as in the
    // console's lead and the roadmap's, and it is deliberately NOT centralised. This banner
    // is the first thing a demo visitor reads and its job is to explain the safety of the
    // demo; those paragraphs explain the product. A shared partial would tie two different
    // purposes together to save one repeated sentence.
    //
    // The safety wording itself stays in this one place, so the three console views cannot
    // drift on what a read-only session means.
%>
<% if (typeof readOnly !== 'undefined' && readOnly) { %>
    <div class="demo-banner">
        This is a <strong>read-only demo</strong> of forgeright. forgeright interviews your team about the
        work they do by hand, then ranks what it finds by what is worth automating first.
        You can look at everything here and change nothing: the buttons that would change
        something are switched off, and nothing you do affects any data.
    </div>
<% } %>
